{"author":{"id":"8d2e0405f08ac6c481f912544d6d5c0f58b2dc14a7e51ad4d499ee7a34567943"},"ops":[{"type":6,"timestamp":1795543746,"nonce":"IjnYcI+oN/Ikb/FOz/nKf2mh8YY=","target":"6a20d3d9c34ba7c90e6c3500e8805a20bdb03bdf80afb1fc681730cb0c2e55d0","message":"`Coupled.__init__` probes the interface stiffness at each participant's stored parameter values. A `jno.np.parameter` with `.initialize(...)` holds 0 until a `jno.core` setup applies the initializer, so a trainable conductivity probed a singular problem (`-0 * Laplacian`). The stiffness came out 0 and the value condition (Dirichlet side, `alpha=\"auto\"`, the floating-subdomain Robin pair) went to the wrong side.\n\n**Reproduce.** `tests/test_coupling_gradients.py::test_a_coupling_probes_a_parameter_at_its_initial_value_before_jno_core_sets_it`: a coupling whose `k_a` has only `.initialize(constant(1.0))` and a `jno.fvm` slab on the other side. Expected: the slab gets the value condition, the stiffnesses are `k/L = 1` and `10`, and the interface value matches the closed form. Before the fix the value went to the fem side. An earlier test hid this by setting the stored value by hand (`eqx.tree_at`).\n\n**Options considered.** Apply the parameter initializer for the probe while the stored value is the placeholder, or probe at `_perturbed_params` as `jno.dd` and fdm `_prepare_steady_slots` do (the ratio of stiffnesses is then not the trained one).\n\n**Fix.** Commit 48cd25b7 in #345 took the first option. In `jno/_coupling/driver.py` the probes give a placeholder parameter its initializer's value (its key, or `PRNGKey(0)`) through `params=`, the traced-input route. No module is changed and no compiled solve keeps the probe's value. A parameter that is set or trained already is probed where it is. The `jno.core` training test in the same file now builds its parameter as a user writes it.","files":null}]}